# Local .terraform directories (contains providers and plugins)
.terraform/

# .tfstate files (contains sensitive system data and secrets)
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Crash logs
crash.log

# Terraform configuration overrides
override.tf
override.tf.json
* _override.tf
* _override.tf.json

# CLI configuration file
.terraformrc
terraform.rc


# Critical Best Practices

# 1. Never Commit to Version Control: Do not push terraform.tfstate or terraform.tfstate.backup to Git. The state file contains plain-text sensitive data, including database passwords, private keys, and cloud API tokens. Always add it to your .gitignore.
# 2. Use a Remote Backend: For team environments, store the state file remotely (e.g., in Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or HCP Terraform). Remote backends provide:
# 	• State Locking: Prevents two developers from running terraform apply concurrently and corrupting the state file.
# 	• Encryption: Protects your sensitive data at rest.
# 3. Do Not Edit Manually: Modifying the raw JSON file directly can easily corrupt your state. Instead, use the built-in Terraform CLI commands to safely manipulate it.

# 🛠️ Common State Management Commands

# • terraform state list – Lists all resources currently tracked by the state file.
# • terraform state show <address> – Shows the detailed attributes of a specific tracked resource.
# • terraform state rm <address> – Removes a resource from the state file (stops tracking it without destroying the actual infrastructure).
# • terraform state mv <source> <destination> – Moves or renames a resource within the state file.
# • terraform import <address> <id> – Brings an existing real-world resource under Terraform management by injecting it into the state file.

#statemanagement s3 creation and locking 

#aws s3api create-bucket --bucket ecom-terraform-state-unique-name-123 --region ap-south-1 --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=ap-south-1
